Z||000000||JohnnyK||04-18-2001||02:07 AM||||I just received a freshly rebuilt master cylinder, adn I have a question about it. When it is not connected to anything (in my hand) and the reservoir is full, and i push the plunger in, should fluid spurt out and drain the reservoir in a couple of strokes? Mine doesn't do it. It spits a bit of clutch fluid out, BUT, if i put my finger of it, and try to push teh plunger, it wont go in (meaning, it is sealing). Whats the dealeo?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||04-18-2001||08:08 AM||||If you are holding your finger over the outlet connection then you shouldn't be able to push the plunger in.  If you push the plunger in with the port open it should spurt a bit of fluid out but not near enough to empty the reservoir.  It would take several strokes to drain it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||04-18-2001||03:24 PM||||Several strokes? it would seem I would need to push it in about a 100 times. But, you couldn't push the plunger in with your finger over the hole. Is there a problem with that?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||04-18-2001||06:23 PM||||There is a valve that only allows so much fluid to be expelled...<BR>The resistance you feel when you seal the hole is normal...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
